{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1090928-adults-in-cofd-innocents","id":1090928,"title":"Adults in CofD Innocents","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-10T17:04:04-07:00","id":1090928,"title":"Adults in CofD Innocents","content":"Just started a new Innocents campaign and its going great but I find it a challenge when having to essentially modify every task based on the perceived age/size/maturity of the character. As there is no direct correlation between children and adults in the game, other than a couple suggested modifiers in combat, as GM I am constantly winging it when the action crosses over from one to another, which is present often.<br /> <br /> In our last session for example the group of kids had to crawl through a broken window high up on an otherwise flat wall. Using Strength and Athletics, and a nasty negative modifier, they managed to help each other and get through. Moments later a pursuing adult arrived. His attributes were actually lower than that of most of the kids but given he was an adult I removed the negative modifier and let him try it straight up.<br /> <br /> I assume this is how these situations should be handled, on a case per case basis with the GM levying some sort of rule/modifier/adjustment based on their perception of how the adult's size/maturity or whatever would effect their performance. <br /> <br /> Another example - Kids witness a guy getting run over by a speeding car - they make shock checks with Resolve and Composure with a negative 2 dice as its a bloody mess. The adult with them however may gross out but is not likely to react as strongly, having a lifetime of gory movies, hospital visits and whatever to form some callouses. Again, I made the attempt easier for him. <br /> <br /> Am I approaching this right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13427-rgrove0172","name":"rgrove0172","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T19:02:02-07:00","id":1090959,"title":"","content":"I would have to double check but I'm sure the book mentions the rule of three.<br /> The modifier your looking for in most cases should be either a negative three for the kids or a comparative positive three for adults.<br /> <br /> Although in one of your examples where the kids escape through the window it's (depending on how you want it) one of those edge cases that might actually be easier for kids. I would flip the static -/+3.<br /> <br /> These situations where kids are better then adults is where innocents shines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/252-basketcase","name":"basketcase","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar252_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T17:32:01-07:00","id":1091349,"title":"","content":"Nope, there is no mention of a 'rule of three' although I suppose Im doing something rather like that. (Maybe a rule of 1 to 3 in my case) and yes your right of course, sometimes the advantage goes the other way such as when the kids ran through a thicket. I levied a -1 die modifier to their travel while the adult cop chasing them got a -3 for the added body mass having to clear through the branches.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13427-rgrove0172","name":"rgrove0172","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}